E('ancient rome|roman empire|romans|rome', 'Ancient Rome grew from a small city in Italy into an empire that ruled the whole Mediterranean world.', 'Rome was traditionally founded in 753 BC; the Western Empire fell in AD 476, and the Eastern Empire lasted until 1453.',
  'It was first a kingdom, then a republic from 509 BC, then an empire after Augustus took power in 27 BC.', 'At its height, around AD 117, it stretched from Britain to Mesopotamia.', 'Romans were brilliant engineers, building roads, aqueducts, bridges and concrete domes.', 'Roman law became the foundation of many modern legal systems.', 'Latin, the Roman language, gave rise to Italian, French, Spanish, Portuguese and Romanian.', 'Christianity became the empire’s official religion in the late 4th century.',
  'Rome’s laws, languages, roads, calendar and ideas of citizenship still shape Europe and beyond.')
E('roman republic', 'The Roman Republic was the period when Rome was governed by elected officials rather than kings or emperors.', 'It lasted from about 509 BC to 27 BC.',
  'Power was shared between two consuls elected each year, the Senate and popular assemblies.', 'Society was divided between patricians, the old aristocracy, and plebeians, the common citizens.', 'The Republic conquered Italy and defeated Carthage in the Punic Wars.', 'In its last century it was torn apart by civil wars between ambitious generals.', 'It ended when Octavian became Augustus, the first emperor.',
  'Its ideas, like checks and balances and the Senate, inspired the founders of the United States.')
E('julius caesar|caesar', 'Julius Caesar was a Roman general and statesman whose rise to power ended the Roman Republic.', 'He lived from 100 to 44 BC.',
  'He conquered Gaul, roughly modern France and Belgium, between 58 and 50 BC.', 'In 49 BC he crossed the Rubicon river with his army, starting a civil war; “crossing the Rubicon” now means passing a point of no return.', 'He defeated his rival Pompey and became dictator.', 'He reformed the calendar into the Julian calendar, the ancestor of the one we use today.', 'He had a relationship with Cleopatra and a son, Caesarion.', 'He was stabbed to death in the Senate on the Ides of March, 15 March 44 BC, by senators including Brutus.',
  'His name became a title: “Kaiser” and “Tsar” both come from Caesar.')
E('augustus|octavian|caesar augustus', 'Augustus, born Octavian, was the first Roman emperor.', 'He lived from 63 BC to AD 14 and ruled as emperor from 27 BC.',
  'He was Julius Caesar’s great-nephew and adopted son.', 'He defeated Mark Antony and Cleopatra at the Battle of Actium in 31 BC.', 'He kept the outward forms of the Republic while holding real power himself.', 'His reign began the Pax Romana, about two centuries of relative peace.', 'He famously said he found Rome a city of brick and left it a city of marble.', 'The month of August is named after him.',
  'He created the system of rule by emperors that lasted for centuries.')
E('punic wars|carthage|hannibal|hannibal barca', 'The Punic Wars were three wars between Rome and Carthage, a powerful trading city in North Africa.', 'They were fought between 264 and 146 BC.',
  'The first war was fought largely at sea over Sicily, and Rome won.', 'In the second war, the Carthaginian general Hannibal marched an army with war elephants over the Alps into Italy.', 'Hannibal crushed the Romans at Cannae in 216 BC, one of history’s most famous battles.', 'Rome recovered, and Scipio Africanus defeated Hannibal at Zama in 202 BC.', 'In the third war Rome destroyed Carthage completely in 146 BC.',
  'Victory made Rome the dominant power of the Mediterranean, and Hannibal is still studied as a master of strategy.')
E('colosseum|the colosseum|flavian amphitheatre', 'The Colosseum is a huge ancient amphitheatre in the centre of Rome.', 'It was begun under Emperor Vespasian around AD 72 and finished under Titus in AD 80.',
  'It could hold an estimated 50,000 to 80,000 spectators.', 'It hosted gladiator fights, animal hunts, executions and public spectacles.', 'Beneath the arena floor was a network of tunnels and lifts called the hypogeum.', 'Earthquakes and stone robbers damaged it over the centuries.',
  'It is one of the most recognisable buildings on Earth and a symbol of Rome itself.')
E('pompeii|vesuvius|mount vesuvius', 'Pompeii was a Roman town near Naples that was buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius.', 'The eruption happened in AD 79.',
  'The town was covered in metres of ash and pumice, preserving it remarkably well.', 'Excavations began in the 18th century.', 'Archaeologists poured plaster into hollows left by bodies in the ash, creating haunting casts of victims.', 'The writer Pliny the Younger left an eyewitness account; his uncle, Pliny the Elder, died trying to rescue people.', 'Nearby Herculaneum was buried too.',
  'Pompeii offers an unmatched snapshot of everyday Roman life.')
E('nero', 'Nero was a Roman emperor remembered as one of the worst.', 'He ruled from AD 54 to 68.',
  'He became emperor at 16, after his mother Agrippina is said to have poisoned his predecessor Claudius.', 'He had his own mother killed in AD 59.', 'A great fire burned much of Rome in AD 64; the story that he “fiddled while Rome burned” is a legend.', 'He blamed Christians for the fire and persecuted them.', 'Facing revolt, he took his own life in AD 68.',
  'His name became a byword for cruelty and excess.')
E('marcus aurelius|meditations', 'Marcus Aurelius was a Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher.', 'He ruled from AD 161 to 180.',
  'He was the last of the “Five Good Emperors”.', 'He spent much of his reign fighting on the empire’s northern frontier.', 'He wrote the Meditations, private notes to himself on how to live well.', 'His Stoic advice focuses on self-control, duty and accepting what we cannot change.', 'His reign also saw a devastating plague, the Antonine Plague.',
  'The Meditations are still widely read as a guide to calm and resilience.')
E('constantine|constantine the great', 'Constantine the Great was the Roman emperor who legalised Christianity.', 'He ruled from AD 306 to 337.',
  'Before the Battle of the Milvian Bridge in 312, he is said to have seen a sign of the cross in the sky.', 'In 313 the Edict of Milan granted religious tolerance across the empire.', 'He called the Council of Nicaea in 325 to settle Christian disputes.', 'He founded a new capital, Constantinople, on the site of Byzantium in 330.',
  'His choices shaped the future of Christianity and of the Eastern Roman Empire.')
E('fall of rome|fall of the roman empire|fall of the western roman empire', 'The fall of the Western Roman Empire was its collapse into separate kingdoms.', 'It is usually dated to AD 476, when the last emperor in the West, Romulus Augustulus, was deposed.',
  'The causes included civil wars, economic trouble, overstretched borders and pressure from migrating peoples.', 'Germanic groups such as the Goths and Vandals settled within the empire.', 'Rome itself was sacked by the Visigoths in 410 and by the Vandals in 455.', 'The Eastern Empire, centred on Constantinople, survived for nearly a thousand more years.',
  'Historians still argue about why Rome fell, and the question shapes how we think about the rise and fall of powers.')
E('byzantine empire|byzantium|eastern roman empire|constantinople', 'The Byzantine Empire was the continuation of the Roman Empire in the east, centred on Constantinople.', 'It lasted from the fall of the West in 476 until Constantinople fell to the Ottomans in 1453.',
  'Its people called themselves Romans, though they spoke mostly Greek.', 'Emperor Justinian I reconquered parts of the West and compiled Roman law.', 'The great church of Hagia Sophia was built under Justinian in 537.', 'It was a centre of Orthodox Christianity.', 'It preserved much classical Greek learning that later reached Western Europe.',
  'Byzantium bridged the ancient and medieval worlds and shaped Eastern Europe and Russia.')
E('ancient china|china history|chinese history', 'Chinese civilisation is one of the oldest continuous cultures in the world.', 'Its first well-documented dynasty, the Shang, ruled from around 1600 to 1046 BC.',
  'China was ruled by a long series of dynasties, including the Zhou, Qin, Han, Tang, Song, Ming and Qing.', 'The Qin dynasty first unified China in 221 BC.', 'Confucianism, Daoism and later Buddhism deeply shaped Chinese thought.', 'China gave the world paper, printing, gunpowder and the compass.', 'The imperial system ended in 1912, when the last emperor, Puyi, abdicated.',
  'Chinese writing, philosophy and inventions influenced all of East Asia and beyond.')
E('qin shi huang|first emperor of china|terracotta army', 'Qin Shi Huang was the first emperor of a unified China.', 'He lived from 259 to 210 BC and declared himself emperor in 221 BC.',
  'He standardised writing, coins, weights, measures and even cart axle widths.', 'He connected earlier walls into an early version of the Great Wall.', 'He ordered books burned and scholars persecuted to silence opposition.', 'He was buried with the Terracotta Army: about eight thousand life-sized clay soldiers.', 'The army was discovered by farmers digging a well in 1974.',
  'His unification created the idea of China as a single state that has lasted ever since.')
E('great wall|great wall of china', 'The Great Wall of China is a series of walls and fortifications built across northern China.', 'Walls were built from the 7th century BC onwards; most of what survives today dates from the Ming dynasty, 1368 to 1644.',
  'It was built to protect against raids by nomadic peoples from the north.', 'Including all its branches, it measures more than 21,000 kilometres.', 'It is made of stone, brick, rammed earth and wood.', 'Contrary to popular myth, it cannot be seen from space with the naked eye.',
  'It is one of the most famous structures on Earth and a symbol of China.')
E('confucius|confucianism', 'Confucius was a Chinese teacher and philosopher whose ideas shaped East Asian society for more than two thousand years.', 'He lived from 551 to 479 BC, in the state of Lu.',
  'He taught that a good society rests on moral leaders, proper relationships and respect for family.', 'Filial piety, respect for parents and ancestors, was central to his teaching.', 'His sayings were collected by his students in the Analects.', 'A version of the “golden rule” appears in his teaching: do not do to others what you would not want done to you.', 'Confucianism became the official philosophy of the Han dynasty and the basis of China’s civil service exams.',
  'His influence on education, government and family life across East Asia is immense.')
E('sun tzu|art of war|the art of war', 'Sun Tzu was a Chinese general and strategist, traditionally credited with The Art of War.', 'He is said to have lived in the 6th century BC, though some scholars doubt the details.',
  'The Art of War is a short book of military strategy in thirteen chapters.', 'It stresses deception, flexibility and knowing both yourself and your enemy.', 'Its most famous idea is that the best victory is won without fighting at all.', 'It has been studied by generals, business leaders and athletes.',
  'It is still one of the most influential books on strategy ever written.')
E('silk road', 'The Silk Road was a network of trade routes linking China with Central Asia, the Middle East and Europe.', 'It flourished from around the 2nd century BC, under the Han dynasty, to the 15th century.',
  'Silk, spices, tea, horses, glass and precious metals travelled along it.', 'Ideas travelled too, including Buddhism, Islam, Christianity, technology and art.', 'Few traders went the whole way; goods passed through many hands.', 'Cities like Samarkand and Kashgar grew rich on the trade.', 'Diseases also spread along it, possibly including the Black Death.',
  'It was one of history’s great connectors of cultures.')
E('ancient india|indus valley|indus valley civilisation|harappa|mohenjo daro', 'The Indus Valley Civilisation was one of the world’s earliest urban cultures, in what is now Pakistan and north-west India.', 'It flourished from about 2600 to 1900 BC.',
  'Its cities, like Harappa and Mohenjo-daro, had planned street grids and advanced drainage systems.', 'People used standard weights and fired bricks of consistent sizes.', 'They had a script that has still not been deciphered.', 'They traded with Mesopotamia.', 'The civilisation declined, possibly because of climate change and shifting rivers.',
  'It shows that sophisticated city planning existed more than four thousand years ago.')
E('ashoka|asoka', 'Ashoka was an emperor of the Maurya dynasty who ruled most of the Indian subcontinent.', 'He reigned from about 268 to 232 BC.',
  'After his bloody conquest of Kalinga, he was horrified by the suffering and turned to Buddhism.', 'He promoted non-violence, tolerance and care for people and animals.', 'He had his edicts carved on rocks and pillars across his empire.', 'He sent Buddhist missionaries abroad, helping spread the religion.', 'His lion capital is now India’s national emblem.',
  'He is remembered as one of history’s most remarkable rulers for choosing compassion after conquest.')
E('buddha|siddhartha gautama|gautama buddha|buddhism', 'Siddhartha Gautama, known as the Buddha, was a spiritual teacher whose teachings founded Buddhism.', 'He probably lived in the 5th century BC in what is now Nepal and northern India.',
  'Tradition says he was a prince who left his comfortable life after seeing old age, sickness and death.', 'After years of searching he reached enlightenment while meditating under a tree.', 'He taught the Four Noble Truths: life involves suffering, suffering has a cause, it can end, and there is a path to end it.', 'That path is the Noble Eightfold Path, a middle way between luxury and harsh self-denial.', 'Buddhism spread across Asia and today has hundreds of millions of followers.',
  'His ideas about mindfulness, compassion and the mind are widely studied today.')
E('persian empire|achaemenid empire|cyrus the great|persia', 'The Achaemenid, or First Persian, Empire was the largest empire the world had yet seen.', 'It lasted from about 550 to 330 BC.',
  'It was founded by Cyrus the Great, who conquered Media, Lydia and Babylon.', 'Cyrus allowed exiled Jews in Babylon to return home, and is remembered for tolerance.', 'Darius I organised the empire into provinces called satrapies and built the Royal Road.', 'Its kings invaded Greece twice and were defeated.', 'Alexander the Great conquered it in the 330s BC.',
  'Persia’s model of a large, diverse, well-run empire influenced many later states.')
